    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with a high-ceiling lineup led by **Trevor Story** (71.87), **James Wood** (70.34), and **Josh Naylor** (68.37). **Trevor Story** is our standout play; he benefits from a favorable 4.56 xERA matchup, a boost from wind blowing out, and a premium 5-hole spot. Meanwhile, **James Wood** is locked in as our table-setter at leadoff; his combination of elite speed and a 4.45 xERA opposing starter makes him a prime candidate for stolen base production despite a slightly negative park factor. **Vinnie Pasquantino** also warrants attention, as he carries a strong 64.21 score, heavily propped up by a 14% efficiency jump provided by his favorable platoon split against Hanifee.\n\nWhile **Tyler Soderstrom** sits at a 67.56 score largely due to a massive +16% park adjustment, we must be cautious with **Seiya Suzuki** (44.29). **Seiya Suzuki** is on a short leash today facing deGrom, whose sub-3.20 xERA suppresses the offensive output of even elite bats, and he remains our lowest-projected starter. **Corbin Carroll** (67.61) remains a high-variance asset; while his speed is vital, we have downgraded his efficiency by 6% due to facing a team with an elite catcher and a high-hold-rate starter, which tempers our expectations for his stolen base contributions.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young**, whose 42.70 score narrowly missed the cut. While he offers some utility, the optimizer favored **Brice Turang** and **Alex Freeland** for their superior order placement and current role consistency. We are also staying disciplined by sitting **Jose Caballero** (39.32), who fell below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor\" due to a brutal matchup against Henderson (2.87 xERA) and a significant platoon disadvantage. Keep a close eye on **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland** as they face Elder; while their scores are middling, they are necessary components of today's defensive alignment.",
